WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.070
Every horror film can't be a masterpiece,
but beyond all those crappy sequels and the

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:09.180
straight-to-DVD dreck, there are a few films
we'd describe as pretty much perfect.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:13.660
From stories about Satanic cults to films
about possessed parents, these horror movies

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.260
are practically flawless.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:17.340
What's more tragic?

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.460
Choosing your own destruction, or being destined
to fail?

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.080
That's the question at the heart of Hereditary.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:27.559
Hailed as the scariest movie of 2018, Hereditary
is certainly frightening, and it features

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.640
one of the most twisted endings in horror
history.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.980
But the film offers a lot more than scares
and screams.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.960
It's a disturbing examination of family life,
the corrosive power of grief, and the crippling

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.620
power of mental illness.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:41.800
"Mom, what are you doing?"

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:48.400
Yes, the film features seances, spirits, and
what might be the creepiest cult ever committed

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:48.940
to celluloid.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.420
Yes, the movie will keep you checking your
ceiling for months to come.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:56.280
And yes, that scene involving a sketchbook
and a fireplace will cause your jaw to hit

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:56.800
the floor.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.200
However, the real power of Hereditary comes
from the sense of foreboding that lingers

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.380
over the entire film.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.320
Just like Toni Collette's character builds
miniature dolls and tiny rooms, it sometimes

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.680
feels like our lives are being manipulated
by unseen forces.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.290
Hereditary seems to suggest that no matter
how hard you try to escape your past, the

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:16.980
traits you inherit will haunt you forever.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.140
If you've ever watched a slasher film, you
know that only virgins get out alive.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.200
If the monster catches you getting intimate,
he'll introduce your face to Mr. Knife.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.850
But writer-director David Robert Mitchell
decided to play with that old cliche in It

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:35.180
Follows, a horror film where having sex can
kill you... but it can also keep you alive.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:40.440
This 2014 film involves a young girl who contracts
a supernatural STD.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:44.439
Unless she passes it along, she'll be killed
by a shapeshifting monster.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.220
"Never go into a place that doesn't have more
than one exit.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.899
It's very slow but it's not dumb."

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.400
If she dies, the creature will hunt down the
guy who gave it to her…and so on and so

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:52.940
forth.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:57.860
It feels like an actual urban legend, but
this film is scarier than any Creepypasta.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.720
That's because the movie monster is one of
the most terrifying in recent memory.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.400
You can't reason with It.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.300
You can't outrun It.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.160
Like death, It just keeps coming.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:07.580
You don't know when.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:08.280
You don't know how.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.440
But rest assured, It will stagger into view
sooner or later.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:15.160
It's as inevitable as growing old, and as
relentless as time.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.890
Directed by Jennifer Kent, The Babadook revolves
around a single mom named Amelia who's dealing

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:24.300
with the death of her husband, plus her extremely
troubled six-year-old son.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.060
Amelia has been trapped in her grief and depression
for a while, and things only get worse when

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:30.380
the Babadook enters the picture.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:35.240
The demon is first introduced to us in the
pages of a pop-up book, and he's the personification

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.420
of Amelia's depression.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:41.320
As she plunges deeper into emotional darkness,
the Babadook grows stronger and stronger.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:45.020
Much like The Shining, The Babadook wants
you to discuss its cryptic ending and try

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:46.640
to figure out what's real and what's not.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:50.480
Is there a literal evil spirit trying to possess
Amelia?

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:51.900
Is it symbolic of her pain?

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:55.840
The best thing about The Babadook is that
the answer is yes and yes.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.240
"You're not my mother."

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.630
"What did you say?"

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.520
"I said, you're not my mother!"

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.980
"I am your mother!"

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:07.680
Horror-comedy is tough.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.180
So many would-be chillers wind up stumbling
over their own goofiness.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.800
But Sam Raimi, the man behind the Evil Dead
franchise, is a master at blending fear and

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:16.320
fun.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.230
Still, not enough horror fans appreciate Drag
Me to Hell.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:24.430
That's a shame, because this 2009 film is
pitch perfect when it comes to melding dark

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.780
humor with genuinely disturbing horror.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:29.240
"You shamed me."

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:34.060
The plot follows a young woman who's been
cursed by a vengeful old witch.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.930
In three days, Christine will be dragged to
hell, and until then, she'll be haunted by

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:38.740
a powerful demon.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:44.220
This simple plot lets Raimi indulge all his
gross-out horror urges, from eyes popping

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.900
out of cakes to wormy corpses.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.720
It's a grotesque, ghoulishly fun romp that
even includes a foul-mouthed, incredibly fierce

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:51.620
old goat.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:04:00.980
But Raimi pulls no punches when it comes to
the scares, and Drag Me to Hell features one

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.900
of the most frightening endings you'll ever
watch through your fingers.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:09.500
Take Ridley Scott's cold directing style and
Sigourney Weaver's badassery, and you've got

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.640
the freakiest sci-fi movie ever made.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:16.600
Released in 1979, Alien is a truly terrifying
experience.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.660
Everything you see on-screen, you can just
about feel on your skin.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:23.220
No matter how you slice it, this is basically
a perfect horror film.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:27.580
Everything in Alien is just so viscous and
violent, and the alien design is... well,

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.260
it's out of this world.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.580
The Xenomorph feels like something that could
exist in real life, and the film brilliantly

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:35.840
exploits our deep-seated fear of parasites.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.599
The idea of a creature crawling around your
innards is truly disgusting, so when the alien

00:04:40.599 --> 00:04:45.250
pops out of Kane's chest, it's still one of
the most visceral horror scenes ever filmed.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.140
But aside from all this monster talk, let's
not forget about Weaver as Ellen Ripley.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.040
"Help me…”

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.880
While she became an icon in the sequel, Ripley
establishes herself as a big-time monster

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.080
hunter in Alien.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.480
Long story short: She's a horror hero for
the ages.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:10.120
Wes Craven's A Nightmare on Elm Street is
a masterpiece of surrealist horror.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:14.780
It's about a supernatural serial killer who
gets you in your sleep, and as the film goes

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:19.520
on, Craven blends the dream world with reality,
leaving you to wonder where waking life ends

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:20.540
and the nightmare begins.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.300
"No running in the hallway."

00:05:24.319 --> 00:05:28.150
With his ratty sweater and demonic glove,
Freddy Krueger is one of the most frightening

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:32.669
monsters to ever haunt the silver screen...
and that's because when you fall asleep, he

00:05:32.669 --> 00:05:33.560
becomes your god.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.620
Just like an actual dream, there are no rules
in Freddy's world.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.530
And when the dreams start bleeding into the
real world, that's scary surrealism at its

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:42.040
finest.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.700
Everyone's scared of getting sick.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.460
Nobody wants to look in the mirror and see
something they don't recognize staring back.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.320
That's why The Fly packs such a punch.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:57.139
Directed by David Cronenberg, this 1986 remake
finds Jeff Goldblum playing eccentric scientist

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.160
Seth Brundle, a man who's morphing into a
hideous insect.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:04.000
Throughout the course of the film, body parts
fall off Brundle's body.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:06.680
He twitches, shakes, and vomits all over his
food.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.220
His humanity is rotting away, and the insect
is taking over.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.740
"No, no,"

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:25.360
Cronenberg has said the movie is an allegory
for AIDS, but the metaphor also works with

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.580
everything from drug addiction to aging.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:29.360
The human body can't last forever.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:33.430
That's why The Fly is sure to linger in your
mind for many years to come.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:35.129
As a wise woman once said:

00:06:35.129 --> 00:06:35.680
"Be afraid.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:37.160
Be very afraid."

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:42.000
There aren't any vampires or werewolves in
The Silence of the Lambs.

00:06:42.009 --> 00:06:45.460
Instead, this Oscar-winning thriller is about
real-life monsters.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:49.340
Hannibal Lecter and Buffalo Bill are terrifying
because they could actually exist.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:53.100
When Lecter stares straight into the camera,
you're looking into the eyes of a genuine

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:56.020
demon... a monster you might actually meet
on the street.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.160
"What is your worst memory of childhood?"

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:01.280
"The death of my father."

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.020
Jonathan Demme's movie is steeped in Gothic
horror.

00:07:05.029 --> 00:07:09.050
Hannibal's dank cell and Buffalo Bill's dungeon
look like lairs you'd find in an Edgar Allan

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:09.620
Poe story.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:14.200
The acting matches the lurid style, with Anthony
Hopkins giving an electrifying performance

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:16.160
as the world's most charming psychopath.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.900
Jodie Foster provides the perfect counterbalance
as Clarice Starling: The character is gritty

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.480
and ambitious — inexperienced, but strong.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:27.800
Of course, murderous cannibals will always
be pretty scary… but the real horror is

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:30.980
the film's depiction of a woman living in
a male-dominated world.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:36.270
Demme uses his unique camerawork and incredible
composition to show Starling surrounded by

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:40.900
intimidating dudes, from squaring off with
a roomful of cops to her nightmare showdown

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:41.800
with Buffalo Bill.

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:46.159
But she never backs down... and her deathly
dance with Hannibal makes for one of the most

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.440
twisted relationships ever seen on the big
screen.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:53.600
Plenty of horror movies provide blood and
guts… but few horror movies actually move

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:54.080
you.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:58.960
When it comes to tragedy, there's no horror
film quite as upsetting as Brian De Palma's

00:07:58.960 --> 00:07:59.480
Carrie.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.340
Underneath all the pig blood and - powers,
Carrie is ultimately a story about a lonely

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:08.039
soul — an outsider who's bullied so badly
she finally breaks.

00:08:08.039 --> 00:08:09.909
"We're all sorry, Carrie."

00:08:09.909 --> 00:08:13.180
"They're all going to laugh at you,"

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.460
"Trust me, Carrie, you can trust me…"

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:19.900
Sissy Spacek gives one of the best performances
in horror history as Carrie White, starting

00:08:20.969 --> 00:08:25.539
off as a terrified teen, morphing into a happy
girl with a great big smile, then turning

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:27.650
into a wide-eyed telekinetic killer.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.580
De Palma is at the top of his game here.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:33.340
He creates a high school world that feels
like something out of a dream…before it

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:35.540
explodes into a full-blown nightmare.

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:40.280
The prom bloodbath is ingenious in its editing
and camerawork, with the director using every

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:43.940
trick in the book — from slow motion and
split screens to lighting techniques straight

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:45.890
out of Italian giallo films.

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:50.480
The result is a movie that's horrific to watch
— because of the fright factor and the loneliness

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:52.520
at the heart of its titular character.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:57.040
Horror fans love to debate which movie was
the first real slasher film.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:59.740
Was it Bay of Blood or The Texas Chainsaw
Massacre?

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.040
Perhaps it was Black Christmas?

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:06.380
Whatever gory flick got things started, Halloween
introduced slasher films into the mainstream.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:11.940
Released in 1978, this brutal little thriller
laid the groundwork for every masked killer

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:16.220
movie that's come since, but few films have
managed to capture the evil essence of the

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:16.840
original.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.120
The moment you hear the Halloween theme, you
want to look over your shoulder to make sure

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.040
someone isn't stalking you.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:32.850
Then there's the chilling visuals, like the
iconic point-of-view tracking shot of Michael

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:34.280
Myers' first kill.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:38.600
Who can forget those brief glimpses of Michael
as he sets his sights on Laurie Strode?

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:42.540
It's all so simple, yet it touches upon something
primal.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:46.320
At the end of the day, Halloween is all about
the man in the mask.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.210
He's the personification of evil, and cinema's
biggest bogeyman.

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:53.570
No matter how many times you kill him, he
keeps coming back for more.

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:54.440
And so do we.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:58.780
Rosemary's Baby is a masterclass in building
suspense and unease.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:00.280
"You're lying!

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:01.820
You witches!"

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:07.080
But instead of using mystery to create tension,
director Roman Polanski lets us know something

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:09.680
devilish is happening pretty much right off
the bat.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.810
Mia Farrow plays Rosemary Woodhouse, a woman
who gets pregnant after a darkly trippy night

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:16.220
that she can't quite remember.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:20.160
Soon after, it becomes clear that something
wicked is growing inside her womb.

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:23.780
Meanwhile, her intensely creepy neighbors
refuse to leave her alone.

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:25.760
"What does your hubby do?"

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:27.300
"He's an actor."

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:32.920
"I knew it, I said to Roman yesterday… he's
so good-lookin'."

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:37.280
At first, Rosemary doesn't understand what's
happening, but the audience sure does… yet

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.320
the lack of mystery never detracts from the
suspense.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:42.180
In fact, it makes it even more unbearable.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:44.690
As Roger Ebert wrote in his review:

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:50.040
"When the conclusion comes, it works not because
it is a surprise but because it is horrifyingly

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:50.580
inevitable."

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:52.840
And what a conclusion it is.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:53.840
"What have you done to it?

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:57.120
What have you done to its eyes?"

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:58.720
"He has his Father's eyes."

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:02.480
The haunted house is a classic horror archetype.

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:07.020
Cinema is full of spooky homes, from The Amityville
Horror to Poltergeist.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:11.470
But few have captured the creeping dread and
terrible sadness of living in a ghostly home

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:12.670
like The Orphanage.

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:16.820
The story follows a woman named Laura who
buys an old orphanage with the hopes of using

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:18.420
it to help disabled children.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:23.140
But when her own son disappears in its darkened
corridors, Laura suspects there might be evil

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.140
spirits at play.

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:28.520
The movie takes its time, jarring you with
disturbing images of a creepy kid wearing

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:29.880
an unsettling mask.

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:35.080
And as Laura searchers for her son and digs
deeper into her past, she learns that history

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:39.520
is full of ghosts, always haunting the present,
and sometimes asking for help.

